Practical No. 14
Global Exception Handling using @ControllerAdvice and @ExceptionHandler
Textbook Reference: Chapter 6 — Building RESTful Microservices
Objective
Centralize exception handling across all REST controllers using @ControllerAdvice, returning a
consistent, structured error response (timestamp, message, status, path) instead of default Spring
stack-trace pages. Built with Spring Boot, Spring Data JPA, Maven, and an embedded H2 database.
Project Setup Summary
Build tool: Maven | Framework: Spring Boot | Database: H2 (in-memory) | Base package:
com.example.api
This practical wires the shared GlobalExceptionHandler into both the Employee module (Practical 12,
now given a proper Service layer so it has somewhere to throw from) and the Customer module (Practical 13,
whose validation errors it now formats). Package layout: exception (shared),
employee.{entity,dto,mapper,repository,service,controller},
customer.{entity,repository,controller}.
Step 3 says to throw ResourceNotFoundException from the service layer instead of returning null. Since the
earlier practicals had the Employee controller call the repository directly, a thin EmployeeService /
EmployeeServiceImpl is introduced here so the lookup-and-throw logic lives in the right layer, as the
instructions require.
