Notes for Testing
• Set header Content-Type: application/json for POST and PUT.
• Also send GET /api/employees/9999 again after the PUT/DELETE tests to confirm all four verbs (GET,
POST-style validation, PUT, DELETE) consistently return structured JSON — never a raw stack trace or
Spring's whitelabel error page.
• Re-run the invalid Customer payload from Practical 13 side by side with this practical's response to see the
difference: the message field now lists every violated field/rule instead of an empty body.
• To exercise the 500 handler, temporarily trigger an unexpected error (e.g. pass a non-numeric id in a path
that expects int can produce a 400 from Spring's own binder; a true 500 is easiest to demonstrate by
deliberately throwing a plain RuntimeException in a test endpoint) and confirm the same { timestamp,
message, status, path } shape comes back with status 500.
• Save all requests into the Postman Collection for reuse.
How to Run
1. Place the files in the package structure shown above.
2. Run mvn spring-boot:run (or run GlobalExceptionApiApplication from an IDE).
3. Employee endpoints: http://localhost:8080/api/employees. Customer endpoints:
http://localhost:8080/api/customers.
4. Open http://localhost:8080/h2-console and connect with JDBC URL jdbc:h2:mem:apidb,
username sa, empty password.
5. Execute the Postman requests: GET missing employee (404) → POST invalid customer (400) → PUT
missing employee (404) → DELETE missing employee (404). Every response should be structured JSON,
never an HTML stack trace.
